# Testing

What the test suite guards, what it needs, and how to run it.

The short version: **this suite tests behaviour, not snapshots.** There are no
`toMatchSnapshot` or `toMatchInlineSnapshot` assertions anywhere in it. Every
assertion either compares rendered bytes against real Ruby Decko output, or
checks that the engine applied a rule it is supposed to apply.

---

## Running the tests

The project's test runs happen on its Linux VM, never on the Windows
development workstation (project decision DEC103). The Ruby reference those
tests compare against is also on the VM: Ruby 3.2.3 with card 1.110.0 and
decko 0.20.0 (project decision DEC102). A result recorded from a Windows run
is not test evidence for this project. On the VM:

```bash
npm install
npm run typecheck
npm test
```

`npm test` runs `node tools/test-gates.mjs run correctness` — the correctness
gate. `tools/test-gates.mjs` assigns every test file to exactly one gate; the
correctness gate holds the product test directories (`tests/card/`,
`tests/cql/`, `tests/server/`, `tests/migrate/` and the others listed there)
and runs on every change. The other gates have their own commands
(`npm run test:merge` for the full suite, `test:parity`, `test:deploy`,
`test:corpus` and so on), and `node tools/test-gates.mjs list` shows them all.

### The two-minute limit

Every test run goes through `tools/testing/run-tests-with-two-minute-limit.mjs`.
The `npm` test scripts call it, and a direct `node tools/test-gates.mjs run
<gate>` re-launches itself under it. The runner:

- sets `DECKO_TEST_PROFILE_FILE` (a fresh file under `.runtime/test-profiles/`,
  or `--profile <file>`). The Vitest reporter
  `tools/testing/streaming-profile-reporter.mjs`, attached in
  `vitest.config.ts`, and the Node runner `tools/run-node-tests.mjs` append one
  JSON line per test file the moment that file finishes, so a killed or
  crashed run still leaves its profile. A Vitest line also names the file's
  three slowest tests;
- kills the whole process tree at 120 seconds, prints the profile so far
  (slowest files first, then the files still running), prints the efficiency
  message and exits with status 124;
- on a run that finishes in time, prints the ten slowest files.

A run that needs more than two minutes is first profiled and made efficient.
If it is already as efficient as it can be, Julian may approve an exception.
An exception is an entry in `tools/testing/two-minute-exceptions.json`, which
only he approves; no agent grants one:

```json
[{ "command": "node tools/test-gates.mjs run full", "limitSeconds": 300,
   "approvedBy": "Julian", "approval": "<his approval, as given>",
   "approvedAt": "2026-11-03T00:00:00Z" }]
```

`command` is the command as typed, with `node` for the Node executable and
repository paths relative to the repository root. The exception applies to
that exact command only. `tests/tools/two-minute-test-limit.test.mjs` fails if
the kill, the streaming profile or this wiring disappears.

Costs the profile has found, and what to use instead:

- **A new PostgreSQL database:** `freshPglite()` from
  `tests/support/fresh-pglite.ts`, not `PGlite.create()`. It loads a dumped,
  freshly initialized cluster instead of running initdb, about six times
  faster, and returns the same empty database.
- **A D1 database per test:** one Miniflare declaring a binding per test
  (as `tests/card/actions/concurrent-saves.test.ts` does), not a Miniflare,
  and so a workerd process, per test.
- **Seeding a file-backed SQLite database:** one `driver.batch(...)`, which is
  one transaction. Statement by statement, every INSERT is its own commit and
  disk sync.
- **One expensive deterministic result checked by several tests:** compute it
  once per file and share it.

> **Native dependency note.** `better-sqlite3` is used by the test harness. It
> ships prebuilt binaries for common platforms. If yours has none, `npm
> install` tries to compile and needs a C++ toolchain. `npm install
> --ignore-scripts` plus a copied prebuild also works.

