## The two corpus tiers

The parity claim is verified by replaying captured Ruby output. There are two
tiers of corpus, and the difference matters when you read a test result.

### 1. The miniature corpus (committed here, not redistributed)

`fixtures/test-corpus/` holds a self-contained sqlite database and captured
shards. It is committed in this development repository, and
`src/testing/test-corpus-paths.ts` resolves to it by default, so `npm test`
here runs the suites that read it; nothing skips for it. **It is not part of
the published package**: the published copy excludes it together with the
suites that read it (see "What the public package does ship" below).

### 2. The rich corpus (NOT shipped either)

The rich corpus is captured from the deck this engine was ported from. It
carries that deck's real content — real card names, real people, real
addresses — which is personal data belonging to someone who did not
authorise its redistribution here.

**It is therefore not part of this fork.** If you have a copy, it belongs
outside the repository.

### What the public package does ship

`npm test` runs the correctness gate (`node tools/test-gates.mjs run
correctness`), and on a clean clone of the published package it runs the
suites whose fixtures are shipped: the from-scratch
synthetic material under `reference-harness/fixtures/` (both `synthetic-v1` and
`write-battery-v1`), `fixtures/deploy-teardown/`, `fixtures/vendor/`, and the
unit tests on synthetic fixtures. The suites that consume the miniature corpus
are excluded along with it, so they neither run nor fail there. The handover
source package is narrower still: its `npm test` is a package smoke check.

The one behaviour, in every copy: a suite whose fixture is excluded from a copy
is absent from that copy; a suite gated on data or setup it does not have
(the rich corpus below) prints `[corpus-gated] NOT RUN` and skips; and a rich
corpus that is requested with `DECKO_TS_CORPUS_V2` but missing fails at import.

